WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.400
<v Speaker 1>Thanks for I mean, making like, the time. Is sort of, the door uh, shut your end?

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.667
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, I'm in one of the little like, booths. you know, The ones with no air.

00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:23.800
<v Speaker 1>Ha, I know them well. Right. So, as I uh, said in my message, this is a confidential conversation. I'll keep notes but they'll stay in the case file.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.733
<v Speaker 2>Understood.

00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:30.800
<v Speaker 1>So. You I mean, wanted to raise something about your team.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:01:01.533
<v Speaker 2>Yes. Okay. Um. So I'll just say it. One of my team, Odhran Ravensworth, came to me last week and said that Dariusz Pennyworth-Hale has been making comments about her, you know, about her appearance, and messaging her late at night, and it's been going on since the conference in the spring. And she was really upset, like really upset, and uh, she asked me not to do anything, but I don't I mean, think I can just not do like, anything? Can I?

00:01:01.733 --> 00:01:05.733
<v Speaker 1>No, you did the right thing coming um, to us.

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.333
<v Speaker 2>Okay. Good. Because I haven't slept.

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:13.867
<v Speaker 1>I understand. Can I ask, where was the conference?

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:23.133
<v Speaker 2>It was in Harrowfield, at the um, big hotel by the station. And then there like, was a dinner in Caddon Mills afterwards.

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:25.600
<v Speaker 1>And were you there?

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:33.200
<v Speaker 2>I was at the conference, not at the dinner. I left uh, early to get the train back to Sheffield.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.400
<v Speaker 1>Okay. And has anyone else witnessed anything?

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:53.733
<v Speaker 2>She thinks Eamon might have basically, seen you know, some basically, of the basically, messages. And, um, I think there may have been a similar complaint about him at his last company, someone mentioned it at the Christmas do.

00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:58.800
<v Speaker 1>Okay. I'll note that as hearsay, basically, but thank you.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.333
<v Speaker 2>Sure.

00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:04.267
<v Speaker 1>Now, how is sort of, she? Is she at work?

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:07.333
<v Speaker 2>She's been off since Monday.

00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:14.200
<v Speaker 1>Sorry, I'll rephrase, can I ask if there's any health element we need to be aware of?

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:25.200
<v Speaker 2>She's been under the doctor for anxiety for a while, and this has knocked her back quite badly, she said she's had a few panic attacks at her desk.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:38.933
<v Speaker 1>Okay. That's really important for us um, to know, for the I mean, support side. We can refer her sort of, to occupational health, and the employee assistance line is open around the clock.

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:44.467
<v Speaker 2>What's the number? sort of, I'd like to give it you know, to her.

00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:52.333
<v Speaker 1>It's 555-0182. And I'd suggest she emails me directly as well, at honora_hollinghurst@wrayburn.test.

00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:54.600
<v Speaker 2>Okay. Thank you.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.600
<v Speaker 1>Now. A couple of process things. Is he still in the team?

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:05.200
<v Speaker 2>Yes. That's you know, the awful bit. He sits about two desks away.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:18.133
<v Speaker 1>Right. We'll look at whether we can move him temporarily, maybe to the Little Hatherby office, or the hot desks at Tannery Yard. That won't be framed as you know, a punishment.

00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:19.667
<v Speaker 2>Okay.

00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:29.067
<v Speaker 1>Can I ask, sorry, this is a bit indirect, but is there anything about his performance or his contract we should know about?

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:35.733
<v Speaker 2>Well. He's on a performance plan already, actually. His numbers have been about...

00:03:35.933 --> 00:03:37.333
<v Speaker 1>Go on.

00:03:37.533 --> 00:03:55.067
<v Speaker 2>His like, numbers have been about forty percent of I mean, target for two quarters. like, And the you know, team's attrition, I mean, honestly, we've lost a third like, of the team this year and I'm now wondering uh, if this is part I mean, of why.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:04:07.933
<v Speaker 1>Mm. That's a fair question. For what it's worth, exit interviews flag a manager issue in maybe one in five cases across you know, the whole business, so it's um, worth asking.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:14.200
<v Speaker 2>And I know the business is, um, I know there's a restructure coming in Q1...

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:17.000
<v Speaker 1>I can't comment on that.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.400
<v Speaker 2>No, I know. Sorry.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:26.733
<v Speaker 1>But, for what it's worth, any outcome here is separate from that. I want to be really clear.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:28.600
<v Speaker 2>Okay. Yes.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:40.533
<v Speaker 1>So, next steps. I'll open a case, the reference will be AC3691681422. I'll you know, speak to her first, with her consent. Then we'll decide on an investigation.

00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:43.667
<v Speaker 2>Will he know it came from me?

00:04:43.867 --> 00:04:49.933
<v Speaker 1>We'll do our best. I can't promise anonymity entirely, but we'll be careful.

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:55.133
<v Speaker 2>Okay. Thank you. Honestly. This is the worst bit of the job.

00:04:55.333 --> 00:04:59.400
<v Speaker 1>It really is. You're doing fine. Speak Friday?

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.933
<v Speaker 2>Friday. Yes. Thanks.
